<Guide lang="en" slug="buying-costs-in-spain" toc={toc} faq={faq} dienst="aankoopkeuring" ctaTitle="Want to know what the property will really cost?" ctaText="With a pre-purchase survey you get a price for everything that needs putting right, before you sign.">
  <h2 id="short">The short answer</h2>
  <p>If you buy a resale home in Andalusia, budget <strong>around 10% on top of the purchase price</strong> for taxes and fees. On a €300,000 house that is roughly €30,000. If the property needs work, and with older homes in the Axarquía that is more often the rule than the exception, add a renovation budget on top.</p>

  <h2 id="tax">Transfer tax or VAT</h2>
  <h3>Resale property: 7% ITP</h3>
  <p>On a resale home you pay transfer tax, <em>Impuesto de Transmisiones Patrimoniales</em> (ITP). In Andalusia this has been a flat 7% since 2021. A lower rate applies in a few specific cases; your lawyer can check whether you qualify.</p>
  <h3>New build: 10% IVA plus 1.2% AJD</h3>
  <p>If you buy a new build directly from the developer you pay 10% VAT (<em>IVA</em>) instead of ITP, plus stamp duty (<em>Actos Jurídicos Documentados</em>, AJD), which is 1.2% in Andalusia.</p>

  <h2 id="notary">Notary, Land Registry and lawyer</h2>
  <table class="prices">
    <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Guide</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Notary (escritura)</td><td>€600 – €1,500</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Land Registry (Registro de la Propiedad)</td><td>€400 – €1,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Independent lawyer</td><td>around 1% + IVA</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Gestoría (tax payments and registration)</td><td>€300 – €600</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Valuation and costs with a mortgage</td><td>€400 – €1,000 + bank fees</td></tr>
      <tr><td>NIE and bank account</td><td>€0 – €200</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note">Guide figures based on typical rates in Málaga province. Exact amounts depend on the price and complexity of the purchase.</p>
  <p>The Spanish notary checks identities and payment, but not whether the property is legal or sound. That is why your own independent lawyer is not a luxury. Choose one who has not been recommended by the estate agent.</p>
  <p>Paying in pounds? A currency transfer specialist usually offers better rates than a high-street bank, and can fix a rate in advance for the completion date.</p>

  <h2 id="example">Worked example: a €250,000 village house</h2>
  <table class="prices">
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Purchase price</td><td>€250,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ITP 7%</td><td>€17,500</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Notary and Land Registry</td><td>€1,800</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lawyer (1% + IVA)</td><td>€3,025</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Gestoría and other</td><td>€700</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Total buying costs</strong></td><td><strong>€23,025 (9.2%)</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>With a mortgage, add valuation and bank fees.</p>

  <h2 id="annual">Annual costs for owners</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>IBI</strong>: local property tax, a percentage of the cadastral value. Usually a few hundred euros a year.</li>
    <li><strong>Rubbish collection</strong> (basura): from a few tens to over a hundred euros a year.</li>
    <li><strong>Non-resident income tax (IRNR)</strong>: if you are not tax resident in Spain you pay tax every year on a deemed income from your home, even if you do not let it. For UK residents the rate is currently 24% on 1.1% or 2% of the cadastral value. If you let the property, different rules apply.</li>
    <li><strong>Community fees</strong> (comunidad): for apartments and urbanisations, from €50 to over €300 a month.</li>
    <li><strong>Insurance, water, electricity and internet</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Maintenance and checks</strong>: an empty house needs attention. See <a href="/en/key-holding/">key holding</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="renovation">Renovation: the forgotten cost</h2>
  <p>Many buyers carefully add up the buying costs and forget that the property also needs work. A thirty-year-old bathroom, wiring without an earth, a leaking roof: these are items costing thousands to tens of thousands of euros. Ask for a survey with repair prices before you buy, and read our guide <a href="/en/guides/renovation-costs-spain/">how much renovation costs in Spain</a>. Buying to renovate and resell? Read <a href="/en/guides/flipping-houses-in-spain/">flipping houses in the Axarquía</a> as well.</p>

  <h2 id="tips">Ways to save money</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Have the property surveyed and use the defects in your negotiation.</li>
    <li>Check the cadastral value is realistic; it determines your annual taxes.</li>
    <li>Leave the plusvalía with the seller, as the law intends.</li>
    <li>Plan the renovation before you move in: working on an occupied house is dearer and harder.</li>
    <li>Use a currency specialist for the transfer from sterling.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="note">This guide gives general information based on the rules in Andalusia in November 2026. Rates can change. It is not tax or legal advice.</p>
</Guide>
